\f0\fs24 \cf0 Nomva Project Update Summary\
\
I made a broad stabilization pass across the app, database pipeline, and Xcode project to get the build and runtime behavior into a much more usable state.\
\
The first group of fixes was in the database pipeline and project setup. I updated the USDA importer so it resolves paths from the repo root, correctly reads USDA's wrapped JSON exports, and writes the generated SQLite file into the app bundle resource path rather than a separate top-level Resources folder. I also corrected the nutrition import math: SR Legacy values are now converted from 100-gram USDA values into serving-sized amounts, and branded foods now prefer USDA labelNutrients so serving calories and macros reflect package-label values instead of inflated per-100g values. In the Xcode project, I added libsqlite3.tbd to the target linkage and kept foods.sqlite in the target's resource phase so the app can actually open the bundled nutrition database at runtime.\
\
The second group of fixes was source-level build cleanup. DatabaseManager was made actor-safe during initialization, its SQLite text binding was corrected, and its FTS search query generation was rewritten to handle common grocery terms like "2% milk" more reliably. SyncManager was simplified onto ObservableObject and @Published instead of the more fragile macro-driven path, and the app plus related views were rewired to use @EnvironmentObject for sync state. I also fixed a number of SwiftUI and SwiftData issues that were surfacing as compiler errors: view previews and query-backed initializers, the editable goals binding needing a ReferenceWritableKeyPath, a bad @Bindable usage in FoodEntryEditView, and actor/concurrency problems in BarcodeScannerView.\
\
The third and largest group of changes was in FoodLoggingService and chat behavior. The app now uses the correct FoundationModels API shape for Apple's on-device model, checks SystemLanguageModel availability at runtime, and returns explicit user-facing reasons when Apple Intelligence is unavailable, disabled, unsupported on the device, or still downloading. I hardened the response parser so raw JSON or fenced JSON does not get shown directly to the user, and I added structured handling for a new ask_clarification response mode so the model can ask one follow-up question instead of guessing. I also tightened the prompt to strongly prefer clarification over silent assumptions whenever portion size, add-ins, preparation, or exact product identity are unclear.\
\
On top of the prompt changes, I added app-side guardrails so logging does not rely solely on the model behaving perfectly. ChatView now dismisses the keyboard on send, passes recent conversation context into FoodLoggingService, and when the assistant has just asked a clarification question, the next user reply is merged with the original meal description before interpretation. That keeps follow-up answers like "it was with 2% milk" attached to the original food instead of drifting into unrelated matches. I also added heuristic ambiguity checks inside FoodLoggingService so even if the model tries to log too early, the app can still intercept vague messages and ask for missing high-value details first.\
\
The result is a much stronger prototype: the database is materially more accurate, the app bundle includes the correct SQLite resources and linkage, CloudKit-related runtime wiring is cleaner, the Apple on-device LLM path fails more gracefully, and the food-logging flow is now more focused on clarification and context retention instead of logging aggressive guesses.\
